{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2193-08-07 23:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Mr. Thompson, I'm Dr. Lee. I understand you came in with a nosebleed that's been going on for a couple of days?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2193-08-07 23:46:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, it started two days ago. It stopped for about half a day, but then came back worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2193-08-07 23:47:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. You went to another ER where they placed a nasal balloon and found your INR was very high—9, correct?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2193-08-07 23:48:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, that’s right. They sent me home, but the bleeding didn’t really stop. I kept feeling blood in my throat."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2193-08-07 23:49:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s why we admitted you. Your INR was 9.8 when you arrived here, which is way too high. That means your blood is very thin and not clotting well."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2193-08-07 23:50:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "But I didn’t change anything—no new meds, no extra warfarin. My last INR was 2.7 just a week ago."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2193-08-07 23:51:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We believe it’s not due to anything you did. Sometimes INR can rise unexpectedly, especially with your medical history."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2193-08-07 23:52:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been feeling dizzy the past couple of days. And I had a headache earlier, but it’s better now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2193-08-07 23:53:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That dizziness could be from the blood loss or the high INR. We’ve given you 1 mg of vitamin K by mouth to help reverse the warfarin effect."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2193-08-07 23:54:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will that bring my INR down quickly?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2193-08-07 23:55:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It helps over the next 24 to 48 hours. We’ve also held your warfarin for now. We’ll check your INR again tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2193-08-07 23:56:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. And the nosebleed—what’s next for that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2193-08-07 23:57:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve consulted ENT. They’ll see you in the morning to look for the bleeding spot and possibly cauterize it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2193-08-07 23:58:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will that hurt?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2193-08-07 23:59:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "They’ll numb your nose first. It might be uncomfortable, but not painful. The goal is to stop the bleeding for good."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2193-08-08 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning, Mr. Thompson. How are you feeling?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2193-08-08 08:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Still feel a little blood in the back of my throat, but the nose isn’t actively gushing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2193-08-08 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s common. ENT is on their way. Your INR this morning is already coming down to 5.5—vitamin K is working."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2193-08-08 08:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good. So I’m improving?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2193-08-08 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. Once we stop the bleeding and your INR is safe, you’ll be on the mend. We’ll restart warfarin when it’s safe."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2193-08-08 12:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Mr. Thompson, ENT successfully cauterized a small bleeding vessel in your septum using silver nitrate. They also applied a material called Surgicell to help it heal."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2193-08-08 12:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Did it work? Is the bleeding gone?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2193-08-08 12:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, hemostasis was achieved. No more active bleeding. You won’t need nasal packing, so no antibiotics are needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2193-08-08 12:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. What about my other meds? I take Plavix too."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2193-08-08 12:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Because of the bleeding risk, we held both warfarin and Plavix. You’ve missed two doses so far. You’ll hold Plavix for five days total, then restart after discharge."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2193-08-08 12:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my aspirin? I take baby aspirin every day."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2193-08-08 12:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re right. Given your history of strokes, we’ve kept you on the baby aspirin the whole time. It’s safer to continue that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2193-08-08 12:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Makes sense. I’ve been dizzy—could that be from the nosebleed?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2193-08-08 12:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Possibly. We’re monitoring you. Your hemoglobin is stable, so no major blood loss. We’ll keep your meclizine for dizziness."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2193-08-08 16:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your latest INR is 3.1—much better. Still a bit high, but trending down. We’ll wait until it’s around 2.8 before restarting warfarin."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2193-08-08 16:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So I’ll go home tomorrow?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2193-08-08 16:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Likely. We’ll check one more INR in the morning. If it’s in range, you can restart warfarin and go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2193-08-08 16:23:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And what about follow-up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2193-08-08 16:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "A visiting nurse will check your INR three times a week and send the results to our clinic. We’ll adjust your dose if needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2193-08-09 08:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news, Mr. Thompson. Your INR is now 2.8—perfect. We’ve restarted your warfarin at your usual dose."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2193-08-09 08:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Great. And Plavix?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2193-08-09 08:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve held it for five days total, including today. You can restart it the day after you go home. We’ll give you written instructions."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2193-08-09 08:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What else should I watch for at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2193-08-09 08:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No nose picking, avoid blowing your nose hard. Weigh yourself daily—call us if you gain more than 3 pounds, given your heart failure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2193-08-09 08:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. Thank you, Doctor. I feel much better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2193-08-09 08:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll discharge you shortly. Take care, and follow up with your primary doctor soon."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2193-08-07 23:36:00",
    "admission_end": "2193-08-09 18:15:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "Mr. Thompson was admitted with epistaxis and a supratherapeutic INR of 9.8, likely due to his warfarin therapy despite no recent medication or dietary changes. He had ongoing oozing and blood in his throat after initial management at an outside hospital with a nasal balloon catheter. He was given oral vitamin K and admitted for monitoring. ENT performed cauterization of a bleeding septal vessel with silver nitrate and Surgicell, achieving hemostasis. Warfarin was held during admission and restarted on discharge when his INR reached 2.8. Plavix was held for five days for safety and was to be resumed post-discharge. His baby aspirin was continued throughout due to his history of strokes. He remained stable, with no further bleeding, and was discharged home with visiting nurse INR monitoring and clear follow-up instructions.",
    "problems": [
      "Epistaxis",
      "Chronic diastolic heart failure",
      "Elevated prostate specific antigen [PSA]",
      "Anticoagulants causing adverse effects in therapeutic use",
      "Congestive heart failure, unspecified",
      "Personal history of transient ischemic attack (TIA), and cerebral infarction without residual deficits",
      "Heart valve replaced by other means",
      "Chronic airway obstruction, not elsewhere classified",
      "Old myocardial infarction",
      "Coronary atherosclerosis of native coronary artery",
      "Diabetes mellitus without mention of complication, type II or unspecified type, not stated as uncontrolled",
      "Chronic kidney disease, unspecified",
      "Long-term (current) use of anticoagulants"
    ]
  }
}
